WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.980
Cross-site scripting, or XSS, occurs when untrusted content executes in a user browser context.

2
00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:12.040
Reflected XSS involves input that is immediately reflected in a response.

3
00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:17.420
Stored XSS involves content saved by the application and later shown to users.

4
00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:24.560
DOM-based XSS happens when client-side code reads data and writes it into the page through an unsafe path.

5
00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:32.180
Output encoding is one of the central defenses because it prevents untrusted data from being interpreted as browser-executable content.

6
00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.460
Encoding is not the only layer.

7
00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:38.720
Input validation can reject values that should never be accepted.

8
00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:44.820
Safe sinks help because they treat data as text rather than markup or code.

9
00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:56.360
Dangerous sinks, such as assigning untrusted content to raw HTML insertion APIs or building scripts as strings, can bypass normal protections.

10
00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:07.100
Content Security Policy can reduce impact in some situations, but it should be treated as a defense-in-depth layer, not a substitute for correct output handling.

11
00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:09.300
Secure framework use matters.

12
00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:27.080
Many XSS issues appear when developers step outside safe framework patterns: disabling escaping, using raw HTML rendering, trusting markdown output, manually manipulating the DOM, or mixing server-rendered and client-rendered content without understanding the boundary.

13
00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:37.660
A good review asks where untrusted data reaches the browser, what context it lands in, which encoder or safe sink is used, and whether any helper bypasses the default protection.
